# C-4 / D-25 — security headers for the STATIC tier.
#
# This file is the platform's static-header file: Cloudflare Pages reads
# `_headers` from the deployed output root and applies it to every response it
# serves. It lives in `ui/public/` because `ui/angular.json` copies
# `{"glob": "**/*", "input": "public"}` into the build output verbatim, so it
# ships without touching the Angular build and without a Pages Function.
#
# NOTE ON COMMENT SYNTAX: this format has exactly one comment marker, `#`.
# A `/* … */` block is NOT a comment here — it parses as a path pattern — which
# is why this file uses `#` throughout. The first non-comment line must be the
# path pattern the headers below it apply to.
#
# WHY BOTH TIERS: the compensating headers were absent from BOTH halves of the
# deployment — the page and the cross-origin API. The API half is
# `server/src/middleware/security-headers.ts`; this file is the page half. They
# are deliberately two files, because they are two tiers with two different
# jobs: this one governs a document that loads and runs script, the other
# governs a JSON endpoint that loads nothing. A test
# (`server/src/middleware/security-headers.test.ts`) asserts the two agree on
# the header NAMES, so a half-fix on either side fails the build.
#
# CONTENT-SECURITY-POLICY: REPORT-ONLY, and why
# --------------------------------------------
# The header below is `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`, not
# `Content-Security-Policy`. The browser evaluates and reports on it WITHOUT
# blocking, which is the staged rollout the owner approved: a policy that has
# never been observed against this application in production is a policy whose
# first enforced day is an outage day. This is an Angular SPA with inline
# styles, `ng-*` bootstrapping, a Milkdown/ProseMirror editor that injects DOM
# the app did not author, and 104 third-party theme stylesheets.
#
# WHAT AN ENFORCING SWITCH WOULD NEED (all four; the full reasoning is in
# `server/src/middleware/security-headers.ts`):
#   1. A REPORT COLLECTOR. `report-uri` below points at a path on this site. If
#      nothing is deployed to receive those reports the report-only phase
#      produces NO EVIDENCE, and flipping to enforcing becomes a guess. Verify
#      reports arrive before switching.
#   2. EVERY DIRECTIVE PROVEN AGAINST REAL TRAFFIC — in particular `style-src`
#      and `script-src`, which carry `'unsafe-inline'` above because Angular
#      emits inline styles and bootstraps `ng-*` elements.
#   3. THE `connect-src` ALLOW-LIST PINNED TO THE DEPLOYED API ORIGIN. The API
#      URL is injected at build time (`API_URL` ->
#      `ui/src/environments/environment.prod.ts`), so it differs per
#      environment and cannot be written down in this file.
#   4. A ROLLBACK COMMIT READY. The switch itself is one header rename; keeping
#      it to a single reviewed commit is the entire point of staging it.
#
# NOT SET, DELIBERATELY: `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy`. The API is a separate
# origin called with a bearer token; a strict CORP here would restrict nothing
# useful and could only break subresource loads.

/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https:; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'; report-uri /__csp-report
